@1 = ~Welcome, child of murder. How many roads have led you here, and how many years have passed since ours last crossed? I wondered whether you would remember me.~
@2 = ~You... have we met?~
@3 = ~Who are you?~
@4 = ~I have no time for mad hermits. The city is burning around us!~
@5 = ~Ah, the young cub who knew nothing of the blood beneath its skin. What began as a little tragedy upon the road from Candlekeep became a revelation written long ago in the Prophecy of Azal'Kahn. I foresaw its unravelling. My premonitions are seldom granted the mercy of being wrong.~
@6 = ~Those storm clouds gathering over Saradush are not the heralds of its end, child. They are only the shadow cast by something approaching. You have mistaken the thunder for the tempest.~
@7 = ~Still the young cub who knows nothing of the blood beneath its skin. What began as a murder upon the road from Candlekeep became a revelation written long ago in the Prophecy of Azal'Kahn. I foresaw its unravelling. My premonitions are seldom granted the mercy of being wrong.~
@8 = ~The measure of a stranger cannot always be taken by his garments.~
@9 = ~It is natural to distrust a face half-hidden by shadows. It is less wise to believe the shadows empty.~
@10 = ~I have watched you for longer than you might bear to imagine: your hasty flight from Candlekeep, the iron cages beneath Athkatla, and now this dying city, which receives you as a wound receives salt. My employers have taken a particular interest in you. The essence of a dead god should have scattered into silence, yet within a mortal vessel it sings, quarrels, and refuses to be still.~
@11 = ~Your coin still stands upon its edge, free for a moment of the fingers of fate. But unseen hands have begun to tilt the table. Saradush is not a trap fashioned for your destruction. It is an incubator, and the world beyond these walls has scarcely begun to reckon with what may hatch here. One day you will choose, and the echo of that choice may travel farther than the stars that shine upon Faerun.~
@12 = ~You are a catalyst, child: one small spark that may set the ages burning. Yet even a fire foretold need not be fed. When the gift is offered, you may accept what others have named your destiny... or find the courage to refuse it.~
@13 = ~W-what are you talking about? Who are you? You speak in riddles, yet act as though you know everything about me...~
@14 = ~Answers are not prizes I may bestow ahead of their hour. Every lock has its season, and every door its cost. Your very essence trembles with power you have scarcely learned to name.~
@15 = ~As does yours, Imoen.~
@16 = ~Peace, Imoen. Your sorrows have not escaped my sight. There is a gentler shore beyond the waters you have crossed. I cannot promise you a life without storms, but the darkest part of your passage draws toward its end.~
@17 = ~There are companions upon your road whose steps have altered the journey more than even they suspect.~
@18 = ~Dear Jaheira. You have stitched your heart about the wound Khalid left, and tell yourself the seam is strong. Yet grief has a memory of its own. I foresaw his death years before it came. There are threads in this world pulled taut by hands no mortal eye was fashioned to discern. Khalid moved among them, as you do, and as I once did.~
@19 = ~(He smiles sadly.) I offer no absolution, Jaheira. Sight and power are not the same gift. Do not let the manner of his passing diminish the truth of his life. Your courage in the shadow of that loss helped guide <CHARNAME> toward a road foretold in the Prophecy of Azal'Kahn. Even a star's light reaches us long after its fire has gone out.~
@20 = ~Yet even the children of Bhaal do not all follow the same road.~
@21 = ~You have already torn one page from the book allotted you, Sarevok. That does not mean you have seen the hand that wrote it. Nor, perhaps, that the book has only one author.~
@22 = ~Child of Bhaal, when the hour comes, remember the instruments already placed within your reach: knowledge, compassion, desire, and the unwritten future. Gorion taught you more than spells and swordplay. My employers have their preferred ending, but I am not permitted to name it. Consider, instead, how strangely the world insists that there can be only one.~
@23 = ~Even the blackest sheep must sometimes be shown a light in the darkness. Take this. A thing which turns what you are into what you might have been. Wear it, or do not. A choice is only a choice when you are free to set it aside.~
@24 = ~Farewell, child of murder. I doubt our paths shall cross again on this side of the veil. But should you ever wonder who has been watching from beyond the window... remember that a window has two sides.~
@25 = ~Another beggar wrapping his purpose in prophecy and pretty nonsense. How tiresome. In the Underdark, such riddles usually concealed a knife.~
@26 = ~Have a care, my friend. His garments belong to a wanderer, yet by Selune's light, the presence beneath them does not. Something about him troubles my spirit.~
@27 = ~This man... there is something wrong about him. Not wickedness, perhaps, but a distance in his gaze, as though he is looking at us from somewhere impossibly far away.~
@28 = ~What? How could you know that? Is this some kind of trick? Did Irenicus leave another one of his lackeys behind to torment us? Because I've had quite enough of that!~
@29 = ~You speak as though you have followed our every step, yet I do not recognise you. I find little comfort in a stranger's philosophy. Who commands your loyalty, and what is it you truly want from us?~
@30 = ~You... knew that Khalid would die? And you stood aside?~
@31 = ~And what of those who have died and risen again? Do the threads of your prophecy bind me as neatly as they bind my sibling?~
